 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Prohibits the application of fail-first or step therapy protocols to coverage for the diagnosis and treatment of mental health conditions.",
 "title": "Relates to prohibiting the application of fail-first or step therapy protocols to coverage for the diagnosis and treatment of mental health conditions",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A3276",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A3276-2021",
     "printNo": "A3276",
     "session": 2021,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": " \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 140\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 3276, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to amend the insurance law, in relation to prohibiting the\n      application of fail-first or step therapy  protocols  to  coverage\n      for the diagnosis and treatment of mental health conditions\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This  bill  would  prohibit  step  therapy  protocols,  which  require\npatients to try a  more  widely  effective  treatment  before  trying  a\nspecific  treatment, in regard to treatment and prescriptions for mental\nhealth conditions.\n \n  I am committed to increasing access to treatment and support  for  all\nmental  health  conditions.  I  want to ensure each patient receives the\ntreatment they need to address the mental or physical health  conditions\nfor which they are diagnosed, and I understand the intent of this legis-\nlation to ensure everyone receives the right care.\n \n  Addressing  discriminatory  step-therapy and \"fail first\" requirements\nfor mental health medications and other treatments are already high-pri-\nority areas for health plan parity compliance in  Department  of  Health\n(DOH)  and Department of Financial Services (DFS) regulations adopted in\n2020, with the consultation and support of the Office of Mental  Health.\nUnder this regulation, each year, New York State regulated insurers must\nattest to their effective internal governance for parity compliance.\n \n  I  continue  to  prioritize parity in the care of mental health condi-\ntions with that of physical health, which is why  I  am  constrained  to\nveto  this  legislation.  However,  there is work to be done to increase\naccess to effective treatments. I therefore direct DFS and DOH to review\nthe step therapy and fail first requirements to ensure that these proto-\ncols, with the approval presumptions in place, are functioning to  guide\nprescribers  to the most effective treatments for each diagnosis without\nburdening the vital work primary care providers perform.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                       (signed) KATHY HOCHUL\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "KATHY HOCHUL",
    "vetoNumber": 140,
    "vetoType": "STANDARD",
    "year": 2022
   }
  ],
